00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.820 align:start position:0%
why do you do what you do what creates a  sense of purpose for you is it working  volunteering being with your family  these are not trivial questions research  shows that having a strong sense of  purpose creates more meaning in life  improves health and leads to greater  satisfaction from work purpose means  intention having a stable far-reaching  goal when we're pursuing our purpose we  feel our lives are more meaningful now  meaning is an abstract concept but  recently behavioral scientists have made  progress toward understanding it better  and even measuring it  I recently conducted a study with my  colleagues roy baumeister kathleen baz  and emily garb in ski where we asked  adults to rate on a seven-point scale  whether their lives were meaningful we  also asked about their beliefs behaviors  and choices those who deem their lives  most meaningful were those that gave the  most to others these same people also  felt the most strongly connected to the  past and future not just the here and  now they had a strong sense of where  they came from and an equally strong  sense of where they're going a purpose  so the feeling of meaning increases when  we feel connected to others and  something bigger than ourselves when we  feel we're part of a story that started  in the past and concludes well into the  future  what's more meaning is not something you  just have or don't have it's more about  your mindset when individuals adopt a  meaning mindset they behave in very  different ways  in one study two researchers Veronica  Hooda and Richard Ryan had half of their  subjects adopt a meaning mindset by  asking them to do one thing each day to  create meaning these activities like  strengthening a social connection  pursuing excellence or helping others  often required effort yet three months  later these subjects reported less  negative moods and more positive  feelings about their social connections  suggesting that meaning can decrease  stress  and boost well-being in the long run as  people find more meaning in their lives  they also tend to get healthier  psychologist Carol riff and her  colleagues demonstrated this in a study  that trapped thousands of people over  time the people living more meaningful  lives by their own account we're  healthier than those who while happy did  not have the same sense of meaning the  high meaning people had higher levels of  good cholesterol healthier weight they  even slept better they have lower levels  of cortisol the stress hormone in their  saliva and where lower risk for  cardiovascular disease and Alzheimer's  disease they also live longer to figure  out why researchers had subjects report  their levels of meaning and then put the  subjects into an fMRI machine then they  show them images of negative things like  crying babies or car accidents the  participants then rated whether the  images were neutral or unpleasant the  brain scans showed that the high meaning  people had less activity in the amygdala  the part of the brain that processes  fear they also reacted more slowly to  the negative images taking longer to  decide whether the pictures were  unpleasant in other words people with  high meaning in life are better able  with the help of their prefrontal cortex  to control their negative emotions  they're better able at putting things in  perspective these findings have  implications for work to people who feel  their work lacks meaning are more likely  to slack off or feel dissatisfied with  their employers the problem is most  people feel this way about their work at  least that was the case in one  large-scale study which tracked over  12,000 employees across a broad range of  categories and showed 50% lacked a sense  and meaning at work the employees who  did derive meaning from their work were  more than three times as likely to stay  with their companies they also reported  1.7 times higher job satisfaction and  were 1.4 times more engaged at work  which brings us back to the original  question why do you do what you do if  you work only for the money chances are  you're less effective than you can be  but if your answer entails meaning your  work helps you become part of a bigger  story and feel connected to others then  it will help you be stronger healthier  and more resilient
 

